Q: How can you implement a game data loading function in Godot using GDScript and C#, and what considerations should be taken into account for object instantiation and setting properties?

A: Implementing a game data loading function in Godot involves reading the saved game data from a file, parsing each line (which represents an object's saved state) back into a dictionary or a Dictionary<string, Variant>, and then using this data to instantiate objects and set their properties accordingly. Here are the steps and considerations in GDScript and C#:

GDScript:
```gdscript
func load_game():
    if not FileAccess.file_exists("user://savegame.save"):
        return  # Error handling: no save file to load
    var save_nodes = get_tree().get_nodes_in_group("Persist")
    for i in save_nodes:  # Clear existing game state
        i.queue_free()
    var save_file = FileAccess.open("user://savegame.save", FileAccess.READ)
    while save_file.get_position() < save_file.get_length():  # Read each saved object
        var json_string = save_file.get_line()
        var json = JSON.new()
        var parse_result = json.parse(json_string)
        if parse_result != OK:
            print("JSON Parse Error: ", json.get_error_message(), " in ", json_string, " at line ", json.get_error_line())
            continue
        var node_data = json.get_data()
        var new_object = load(node_data["filename"]).instantiate()  # Instantiate object
        get_node(node_data["parent"]).add_child(new_object)  # Set parent
        new_object.position = Vector2(node_data["pos_x"], node_data["pos_y"])  # Set position
        for i in node_data.keys():  # Set other properties
            if i in ["filename", "parent", "pos_x", "pos_y"]:
                continue
            new_object.set(i, node_data[i])
```

C#:
```csharp
public void LoadGame()
{
    if (!FileAccess.FileExists("user://savegame.save"))
    {
        return;  // Error handling: no save file to load
    }
    var saveNodes = GetTree().GetNodesInGroup("Persist");
    foreach (Node saveNode in saveNodes)  # Clear existing game state
    {
        saveNode.QueueFree();
    }
    using var saveFile = FileAccess.Open("user://savegame.save", FileAccess.ModeFlags.Read);
    while (saveFile.GetPosition() < saveFile.GetLength())  # Read each saved object
    {
        var jsonString = saveFile.GetLine();
        var json = new Json();
        var parseResult = json.Parse(jsonString);
        if (parseResult != Error.Ok)
        {
            GD.Print($"JSON Parse Error: {json.GetErrorMessage()} in {jsonString} at line {json.GetErrorLine()}");
            continue;
        }
        var nodeData = new Godot.Collections.Dictionary<string, Variant>((Godot.Collections.Dictionary)json.Data);
        var newObjectScene = GD.Load<PackedScene>(nodeData["Filename"].ToString());
        var newObject = newObjectScene.Instantiate<Node>();  # Instantiate object
        GetNode(nodeData["Parent"].ToString()).AddChild(newObject);  # Set parent
        newObject.Set(Node2D.PropertyName.Position, new Vector2((float)nodeData["PosX"], (float)nodeData["PosY"]));  # Set position
        foreach (var (key, value) in nodeData)  # Set other properties
        {
            if (key == "Filename" || key == "Parent" || key == "PosX" || key == "PosY")
            {
                continue;
            }
            newObject.Set(key, value);
        }
    }
}
```
When implementing a loading function, consider the following:
- Ensure no existing game state conflicts with the loaded state. This may involve clearing or resetting the current game state before loading.
- Instantiate each object based on its saved filename and add it to the correct parent in the scene tree.
- Set each object's properties according to the saved data, excluding special properties like filename or parent, which are used for instantiation and hierarchy setup.
- Handle errors gracefully, such as missing save files or JSON parse errors, to avoid game crashes during the load process.

